{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful figure in the 12th century Mediterranean world a prominent ruler of the County of Tripoli. He served as Count from 1130 to 1144 and as Prince of Antioch from 1139 to 1144. His reign was marked by military campaigns political maneuvering and significant territorial expansion.\n\n*Early Life and Ascendancy* Raymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1130. He quickly consolidated his power facing challenges from rival factions and neighboring powers. He skillfully navigated complex alliances and often relied on military force to secure his position. Raymond's early rule focused on strengthening the defenses of Tripoli and expanding its influence in the Levant. He fostered trade and developed a strong military force essential for protecting the region from external threats.\n\nRaymond's ambition extended beyond the County of Tripoli. He sought to control Antioch a strategically important territory coveted by various powers including the Byzantine Empire and the Seljuk Turks. He successfully asserted his claim to Antioch in 1139 becoming Prince of Antioch. This position significantly enhanced his prestige and power in the region. His rule in Antioch was characterized by military campaigns against the Seljuk Turks and efforts to maintain stability within the territory.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III was a renowned military leader. He led numerous campaigns against the Seljuk Turks who posed a constant threat to the Levant. His military successes earned him considerable respect and solidified his reputation as a formidable warrior. He also engaged in conflicts with other neighboring powers such as the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. These conflicts were often driven by territorial disputes and competition for influence. Raymond's military campaigns were not always successful but he consistently demonstrated his ability to defend his territories and project power. He employed innovative military tactics and maintained a well-trained army.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III's reign was a period of significant growth and stability for the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Antioch. He expanded the territories under his control strengthened the defenses and fostered economic prosperity. His leadership was instrumental in maintaining a relatively independent power in the face of powerful external forces. However his reign ended tragically in 1144. He was killed during the Siege of Antioch by the Crusaders. His death marked a significant turning point in the history of the region and left a void in the political landscape. Despite his untimely death Raymond III's legacy as a powerful and influential ruler of the Levant endures. He is remembered as a skilled military leader a shrewd politician and a defender of the Christian territories in the Holy Land.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.14342188835144043}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.5060096454108134e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0798519179224968}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.04111293703317642}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5501455068588257}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -1.6038830280303955}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.024912608787417412}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.003749603871256113}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.6708590984344482}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0015584002248942852}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.04174918308854103}, {"token": "▁Mediterranean", "logprob": -1.1756385564804077}, {"token": "▁world", "logprob": -0.0005523824